+# BLOCKED: `002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to production
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` makes `users.email` required (`NOT NULL`) and
+case-insensitively unique. It works on a clean database, but it cannot be
+applied to the current production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`)
+without changing customer rows.
+
+## Why
+
+The production snapshot violates both new constraints:
+
+- **NULL email:** id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL`.
+- **Exact duplicate:** ids 1 and 23 both have `ana.silva@example.com`.
+- **Case-insensitive duplicate:** ids 1 and 4 have `ana.silva@example.com` and
+  `Ana.Silva@example.com`.
+
+SQLite cannot add `NOT NULL` to an existing column in place, so the migration
+rebuilds `users`. Inserting the existing rows into the rebuilt table then fails
+on the `NOT NULL` and unique constraints. The only ways to make that succeed
+are to update, merge, or delete the offending rows, or to leave production
+unenforced (for example, only preventing new writes with triggers or a partial
+index). The task explicitly forbids modifying or deleting customer rows because
+Support owns that data, so this is not something the migration may do.
+
+## What is implemented
+
+`002_unique_email.sql` is complete for a clean database. It rebuilds `users`
+with `email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE` while preserving `id` and
+`name`.
+
+Before rebuilding, it runs a read-only preflight. If any NULL or
+case-insensitive duplicate emails exist, it aborts with an explicit error
+before touching `users`; no customer rows are changed or removed. Against the
+current snapshot, that preflight is what fires.
+
+As a result:
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` still fails with the preflight abort against
+  the current snapshot. That is the block, not a defect in the test.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must decide how to clean or canonicalize the data, for example:
+
+- choose an email for id 17 (or otherwise resolve that account);
+- resolve ids 1/4 and 1/23 according to Support's duplicate-account policy.
+
+Once the snapshot contains no NULLs or case-insensitive duplicates, re-running
+the migration preserves every remaining row and installs the required
+constraints.
diff --git a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
index 91e11a9..597f71d 100644
--- a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
+++ b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
@@ -1,2 +1,52 @@
 -- 002: users.email must be present and unique, ignoring case.
--- TODO
+--
+-- SQLite cannot add a NOT NULL constraint to an existing column in place, so
+-- the table has to be rebuilt. Before doing that, check the existing rows and
+-- abort without touching users if the data already violates the new
+-- constraints. See BLOCKED.md: the current production snapshot has both NULL
+-- and duplicate emails, and Support owns that data.
+CREATE TEMP TABLE _users_email_precheck (violation TEXT);
+
+CREATE TEMP TRIGGER _users_email_precheck_abort
+BEFORE INSERT ON _users_email_precheck
+BEGIN
+    SELECT RAISE(
+        ABORT,
+        '002_unique_email: users.email has NULL values or case-insensitive duplicates; Support must clean the data first (see BLOCKED.md)'
+    );
+END;
+
+INSERT INTO _users_email_precheck (violation)
+SELECT 'email is NULL'
+FROM users
+WHERE email IS NULL
+UNION ALL
+SELECT 'case-insensitive duplicate email'
+FROM users u
+WHERE u.email IS NOT NULL
+  AND EXISTS (
+      SELECT 1
+      FROM users v
+      WHERE v.email IS NOT NULL
+        AND v.email = u.email COLLATE NOCASE
+        AND v.id <> u.id
+  )
+LIMIT 1;
+
+DROP TABLE _users_email_precheck;
+
+BEGIN;
+
+CREATE TABLE users_rebuilt (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE,
+    name TEXT
+);
+
+INSERT INTO users_rebuilt (id, email, name)
+SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_rebuilt RENAME TO users;
+
+COMMIT;
